A||||3||GT-AutoX||Why can't I find the right coolant temp sensor for 86.5 GT?||||11||
Z||000000||GT-AutoX||05-29-2001||09:02 PM||||I recently bought an 86.5 GT from a budget used car lot (don't worry, I traded in an equally sketchy car for it).  It's a 5spd showing 97K.  The socket on my coolant temperature sensor is broken off (near waterpump connected to ecm) and for some reason I can't find the correct replacement anywhere.  The parts stores keep giving me one with the correct plug and socket size, but the wrong thread diameter.  I even asked for different model years of 2.8's and they're all the same.  The one in my car has about a 1" thread diameter, a metal probe recessed inside the threaded region, and takes a 3/4" socket.  The "replacement" has about a 3/4" thread diameter, a metal probe that sticks out beyong the threaded region, and also takes a 3/4" socket.  I guess either my block's wrong or it's a really obscure model year variation.||||reg||11
Z||000001||Seanh||05-29-2001||09:57 PM||||Did you just try the new style one? It might work. I recall having some trouble with this a few years back, but don't recall what I did. Im pretty sure the new style worked fine for me.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Two88GTs||05-30-2001||12:38 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GT-AutoX:<BR><B>I recently bought an 86.5 GT from a budget used car lot (don't worry, I traded in an equally sketchy car for it).  It's a 5spd showing 97K.  The socket on my coolant temperature sensor is broken off (near waterpump connected to ecm) and for some reason I can't find the correct replacement anywhere.  The parts stores keep giving me one with the correct plug and socket size, but the wrong thread diameter.  I even asked for different model years of 2.8's and they're all the same.  The one in my car has about a 1" thread diameter, a metal probe recessed inside the threaded region, and takes a 3/4" socket.  The "replacement" has about a 3/4" thread diameter, a metal probe that sticks out beyong the threaded region, and also takes a 3/4" socket.  I guess either my block's wrong or it's a really obscure model year variation.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey there, it sounds as if you need to reuse the threaded adaptor from your old sensor, refer to: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010037.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010037.html</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR>88GT 66k<BR>88GT 59k||||reg||1
Z||000003||Devotshka||05-30-2001||11:10 PM||||Take two ratchets, and put em on either nut, then twist, the sleeve will come off for reuse on the new sensor   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Devotshka (edited 05-30-2001).]||||reg||1
